 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of fragments generated by rasterization. Single pass makes it extremely fast [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H \times D)$ bits for the voxel grid [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Highly efficient on modern GPUs due to single-pass nature and simple bitwise operations [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ fragments processed in a single pass [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** One bit per voxel in the grid, stored in a texture array or 3D texture [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Single-pass rasterization with bitwise accumulation [S1].
 *   **Strengths:** Fast, single-pass, produces solid interiors, no aliasing from XOR operator [S1].
 *   **Limitations:** Requires watertight geometry; sensitive to cracks and nested shells; requires Depth Clamp; limited to view-aligned voxelization; DX9 requires texture tiling [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When fast voxelization of watertight models is needed and view-aligned grids are acceptable.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For non-watertight models, models with internal voids that should be filled, or when arbitrary voxel grid orientations are required.
+*   **When to Use:** When fast voxelization of watertight models is needed and view-aligned grids are acceptable [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For non-watertight models, models with internal voids that should be filled, or when arbitrary voxel grid orientations are required [G].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the paper "Single-pass GPU Solid Voxelization and Applications" [S1]. The method leverages the Jordan curve theorem, similar to techniques used in shadow volume rendering [S1]. It was developed to provide a fast, GPU-based solution for solid voxelization, addressing the limitations of previous multi-pass or CPU-based methods.
